import "core/list.ct"
import "core/render.ct"


# # The list library's displays of its own calls
  A display of a call says what a function did, where a display of a value says what the function left
  behind. @core/list.ct@'s combinators are the case for it: each of them returns a list, and a list is
  the one thing about the call a reader could already see. What they cannot see is the correspondence —
  which element became which, which was kept, what each step added to the accumulator — and that is the
  whole of what the combinator does.
  Each is declared by an arm of [/Catln/Doc/dshowCall] whose argument is a call of the function. There is
  no annotation marking the function and no name derived from it: the arm is the declaration, as an arm
  of [/Catln/Doc/dshow] is for a type. The arms here are bounded to [/Data/List/ConsList] so they are the
  list's displays and not every container's — a [/Data/Maybe] mapped over looks nothing like a table of
  rows and would say so in a page of its own.
# # Why this is a page of its own
  The arms name [/Catln/Doc/dshowCall], so this page reaches @core/webdoc.ct@, and @core/list.ct@ is
  reached by nearly every page there is. Writing them there would put the doc server's vocabulary
  underneath the list library. So this sits where @core/render.ct@ and @core/result.ct@ sit and by the
  same arrangement: it reaches @core/list.ct@ freely, and nothing in the library reaches it.
module(Data/List)
  # [fmap] over a [ConsList] shown as the table of what each element became: a row per element, the
    element on the left and the result of applying [fn] to it on the right.
    This is the display of the call and not of the list it returns. The recursion that @core/list.ct@'s
    instance is written as is the generic display — a call per element, each holding a shorter list than
    the one above it — and it is a display of how the definition is written rather than of what it does.
    The table is the second, and is what a reader opening a mapping wants.
    Bounded to elements that are [/Data/ToString], which is what a row can hold (see
    [/Catln/Doc/Show/Step]). A mapping over elements that render no text does not match this arm and is
    shown the way it was before there was one.
  # A mapping over a [ConsList] of things that render text is a call with a display, which is what
    joining [/Catln/Doc/DShowCall] says. The bound on the elements is the whole of which mappings this
    is claimed for: a mapping over elements that render no text is not a member and keeps the display
    every other call gets.
  every(/Data/Functor/fmap(lst -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], fn(v)), isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShowCall])

  /Catln/Doc/dshowCall(s= /Data/Functor/fmap(lst -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], fn(v)))= /Catln/Doc/Show/Steps(steps= fmapSteps(lst= lst, fn(v)= fn(v= v), index= 0))

  # The rows of that table, walking the list and applying [fn] to each element as it goes.
    It applies [fn] rather than being handed what [fn] already returned. An arm of
    [/Catln/Doc/dshowCall] is given the call's arguments and nothing else, so working the outputs out is
    the show's own job — which is also what lets the arm above say what a mapping does without the
    debugger arranging anything for it.
    [index] is threaded rather than derived, since a [ConsList] knows nothing about where in a list it
    sits, and the rows are wanted numbered from the front.
  fmapSteps(lst -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], fn(v -> $_A) -> $_B, index -> Integer) -> /Data/List[$T -> /Catln/Doc/Show/Step..]
  fmapSteps(lst= Nil, fn(v), index)= Nil
  fmapSteps(lst= Cons(head, tail), fn(v), index)= Cons(head= /Catln/Doc/Show/Step(index= index, input= head.toString, output= fn(v= head).toString), tail= fmapSteps(lst= tail, fn(v)= fn(v= v), index= index + 1))

  # [/Data/Monad/mmap] over a [ConsList] shown as the same table as a mapping: a row per element, the
    element on the left and the list [fn] made of it on the right.
    The result of the call is those lists joined end to end, and joined they no longer say which
    element contributed what — or, where an element contributed the empty list, that it was there at
    all. The table keeps each element beside its own contribution.
    This is also how a [filter] is shown. [filter] is a single call of [mmap] whose function makes a
    one-element list of what it keeps and the empty list of what it drops, so the call of [mmap]
    inside it reads as the filter's verdicts: a kept element beside a list holding it, a dropped one
    beside the empty list. The filter is not given a display of its own, since it would be this one
    again with nothing added.
    Bounded as the mapping is, to elements that are [/Data/ToString], and to [ConsList] so a
    [/Data/Maybe] bound over keeps the display of any other call.
  # A binding over a [ConsList] of things that render text is a call with a display.
  every(/Data/Monad/mmap(m -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], fn(v)), isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShowCall])

  /Catln/Doc/dshowCall(s= /Data/Monad/mmap(m -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], fn(v)))= /Catln/Doc/Show/Steps(steps= mmapSteps(m= m, fn(v)= fn(v= v), index= 0))

  # The rows of that table, applying [fn] to each element as it goes, as [fmapSteps] does.
  mmapSteps(m -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], fn(v -> $_A) -> $_B, index -> Integer) -> /Data/List[$T -> /Catln/Doc/Show/Step]
  mmapSteps(m= Nil, fn(v), index)= Nil
  mmapSteps(m= Cons(head, tail), fn(v), index)= Cons(head= /Catln/Doc/Show/Step(index= index, input= head.toString, output= fn(v= head).toString), tail= mmapSteps(m= tail, fn(v)= fn(v= v), index= index + 1))

  # The rows written out as text, which is how this page checks the ones it builds.
    It is not an arm of [/Data/toString] and [/Catln/Doc/Show/Steps] is not a [/Data/ToString]: a value
    that renders text is shown as that text, so making these rows one would replace the display they
    exist to be. So this is a rendering the page keeps to itself.
    A test and not a golden, because the arm above is reached only from a debugger and so cannot be
    written into a [#print] here. What a [#print] could reach is [fmapSteps], and what it builds is
    everything the arm does beyond handing it over — so that is what is asserted.
  showSteps(steps -> /Data/List[$T -> /Catln/Doc/Show/Step..]) -> String
  showSteps(steps= Nil)= ""
  showSteps(steps= Cons(head= /Catln/Doc/Show/Step(index, input, output), tail))= index.toString ++ ":" ++ input ++ "->" ++ output ++ ";" ++ showSteps(steps= tail)

  exampleFmapSteps =
    #example
    #assert(test= showSteps(steps= fmapSteps(lst= [1, 2, 3], fn(v)= v + 1, index= 0)) == "0:1->2;1:2->3;2:3->4;")
    #assert(test= showSteps(steps= fmapSteps(lst= [], fn(v)= v + 1, index= 0)) == "")
    0

  exampleMmapSteps =
    #example
    #assert(test= showSteps(steps= mmapSteps(m= [1, 2], fn(v)= [v, v], index= 0)) == "0:1->[1, 1];1:2->[2, 2];")
    #assert(test= showSteps(steps= mmapSteps(m= [1, 2, 3], fn(v)= [], index= 0)) == "0:1->[];1:2->[];2:3->[];")
    #assert(test= showSteps(steps= mmapSteps(m= [], fn(v)= [v], index= 0)) == "")
    0
